# Erdős Problem #99 — AG58 propagation through the Type-II scalar and defect ledgers

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Target:** Target C-II canonical full-core Type-II branch  
**Claim class:** proof-only propagation of AG58  
**External input:** none  
**Computation:** exact-rational endpoint certificate for Section 171

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The weighted-minority allocation refinement proves the proof-only atomic gain

\[
\eta_{grid}=0.00058.
\]

This note propagates that change through the existing correlated stopped-train, Section-171, chamber-interface, rhombus-deficit, and residual-budget ledgers. It introduces no new geometry beyond AG58.

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
s_2=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad
\rho=0.7390851332151606\ldots,
\]

where `rho=cos(rho)`, and

\[
C_{146}={4\over9}(\pi+s_2).
\]

---

## 1. Correlated stopped-train split

The exact identity

\[
\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R-q
=2\left(T-{q\over2}-2\sqrt{R_4}\right)
\]

and AG58 give

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R-q\over D}\ge0.00116.
}
\tag{P58.1}
\]

Using `q/D>=2rho-o(1)`,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R\over D}
\ge2\rho+0.00116
=1.479330266430321\ldots .
}
\tag{P58.2}
\]

Hence at least one of

\[
\boxed{
S={\rho_{rec}\over2}
\ge(\rho/2+0.00029-o(1))D
=0.369832566607580\ldots D-o(D),
}
\tag{P58.3}
\]

or

\[
\boxed{
\sigma_R\ge(\rho+0.00058-o(1))D
=0.739665133215160\ldots D-o(D)
}
\tag{P58.4}
\]

must hold.

On the rhombic branch,

\[
M_R={\sigma_R\over8}
\left(4\sqrt{R_4}+{\sigma_R\over2}\right)
\]

gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {M_R\over D^2}
\ge {\rho+0.00058\over8}
\left(4s_2+{\rho+0.00058\over2}\right)
=0.386390321406654\ldots .
}
\tag{P58.5}
\]

The exact post-endpoint scalar identity improves to

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\mu_D
\ge\mu_{TC}+{\rho\over5}+{0.00058\over5}
=1.785495210824532\ldots .
}
\tag{P58.6}
\]

The direct correlated endpoint/interface floor is

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {J\over D}
\ge2\rho+4s_2-\pi+0.00116
=2.146988840296358\ldots .
}
\tag{P58.7}
\]

---

## 2. Section-171 endpoint with `g=0.00058`

Audited Section 171 reduces every Type-II subsequential limit

\[
t=\lim{q-k\over D},
\qquad
\delta=\lim{b-P_F\over D}\ge0
\]

to

\[
F_t(\delta)=
10t-5\pi-5\delta
+\left(81(\pi+\delta)^2\delta\right)^{1/3}
-{8\Delta_2\over9t/2-2\pi-2\delta}
\ge0.
\tag{P58.8}
\]

With train gain `g`, the feasible detour interval is

\[
0\le\delta\le
{{9\over2}(t-C_{146})-g\over2}.
\tag{P58.9}
\]

Set

\[
g=0.00058,
\qquad
\varepsilon_{58}=0.0014085,
\qquad
t_{58}=C_{146}+\varepsilon_{58}.
\tag{P58.10}
\]

Then the right endpoint is

\[
\delta_{58}=0.002879125.
\tag{P58.11}
\]

The exact-rational certificate verifies:

1. `F_{t58}` is strictly increasing throughout the feasible detour interval;
2. `F_{t58}(delta58)<0` with positive outward-rounding margin; and
3. the Section-171 derivative in `t` is positive on the same domain.

Therefore no feasible Type-II limit has `t<=t58`, and

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {q-k\over D}
\ge C_{146}+0.0014085
=1.820922037979444\ldots .
}
\tag{P58.12}
\]

A direct numerical solve of the unchanged endpoint equation gives an offset about

\[
0.0014087787596,
\]

so `0.0014085` keeps a nontrivial safety margin. Further decimal tuning of Section 171 remains low-value.

---

## 3. Mandatory chamber/interface floor

AG58 gives

\[
{T-q/2\over D}\ge2s_2+0.00058-o(1).
\]

Using the exact identity `J=2T-P_F`, `P_F/D->pi`, and (P58.12),

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {J\over D}
\ge j_{58}:=t_{58}+2(2s_2+0.00058)-\pi
=2.489740611845480\ldots .
}
\tag{P58.13}
\]

The exact chamber decomposition

\[
\Sigma_{ch}=J+a+L_{add}+R_F
\]
therefore gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\Sigma_{ch}\over D}
\ge2.489740611845480\ldots .
}
\tag{P58.14}
\]

As before, this is mandatory boundary/interface credit, not genuine repacking spare.

---

## 4. Exact `H` bootstrap and linear rhombus deficit

The exact relation

\[
H={J\over2}+(q-k)-{P_F\over2}
\]

with `P_F/D->pi` gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {H\over D}
\ge h_{58}:={j_{58}\over2}+t_{58}-{\pi\over2}
=1.494996017107288\ldots .
}
\tag{P58.15}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {A_{ch}-a_0Z_{ch}\over D}
\ge {\sqrt3\over4}h_{58}
=0.647352264685733\ldots,
}
\tag{P58.16}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\Delta_2D^2-R_4\over D}
\ge {h_{58}\over2}
=0.747498008553644\ldots .
}
\tag{P58.17}
\]

---

## 5. Corrected residual budget

The finite corrected residual cap remains

\[
R_F+\Sigma_{ext}
<{P_K\over2}+P_F-q-{\delta_K+E_4\over a_0}.
\]

Using `q>=q-k`, `P_K,P_F<=pi D`, and (P58.12),

\[
\boxed{
\limsup {R_F+\Sigma_{ext}\over D}
\le {3\pi\over2}-t_{58}
=2.891466942405245\ldots .
}
\tag{P58.18}
\]

Thus AG58 moves the two relevant first-order quantities in opposite directions:

- mandatory chamber/interface complexity rises;
- genuine chamber-plus-exterior residual allowance falls.

The gap is still not contradictory.

---

## 6. Current geometric split

At proof-only scope, every full-core survivor now lies in at least one of the following two mechanisms:

```text
singleton branch:
    S >= 0.3698325666 D - o(D);

rhombic branch:
    sigma_R >= 0.7396651332 D - o(D),
    M_R >= 0.3863903214 D^2 - o(D^2).
```

The scalar envelope is no longer the sensible target. The remaining work should use geometry of singleton stopped trains, or the quadratic missing-crossing structure in the rhombic branch.

The exact Section-171 certificate is `research/verify_target_c_ag58_global_propagation.py`.

**Disposition:** proof-only AG58 propagation. C-II and Level I remain open.
